<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>Scheduler</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Scheduler</AssemblyName>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>

    <!--
      No web SDK and no ASP.NET Core reference, unlike samples/workflow. This application
      has no inbound transport at all: nothing calls it, there is no route to map and no
      principal to authenticate. A cron expression starts it, which is the claim the
      sample exists to make, and adding an HTTP host to prove that would be adding the
      thing whose absence is the point.

      NativeAOT is off for samples/workflow's reason: the flow declares Durable, a durable
      flow is refused without a journal, and the only journal FlowX ships is PostgreSQL —
      so this project references Npgsql. samples/ecommerce stays the AOT arm.
    -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>false</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The in-memory ledger, bank and report desk are internal: they are the fixture, not
      the sample's story. The test project sees them rather than this project widening
      its surface to be testable — the bargain samples/workflow strikes.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Scheduler.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The generic host, not the web one. FlowX.Hosting takes only Hosting.Abstractions —
      a library takes the contract, not the container — so an application that runs
      FlowX's background sweeps has to bring an implementation, and this is the smallest
      one that is not a web server.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" Version="10.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/FlowX.Abstractions/FlowX.Abstractions.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/FlowX.Core/FlowX.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/FlowX.Runtime/FlowX.Runtime.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/FlowX.Hosting/FlowX.Hosting.csproj" />

    <!--
      The journal, and the reason this sample cannot start without a database. A schedule
      fires once across a fleet because flow_instance's primary key refuses every node but
      one; with no journal there is no primary key, and FlowScheduleScan.IsEnabled is false
      so the node sweeps for nothing at all.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../../plugins/FlowX.Postgres/FlowX.Postgres.csproj" />

    <!--
      The compiler as an ANALYZER, so the generator runs inside this project's build
      against this project's source. It is what turns the [CronTrigger] below into
      AddFlowXSchedules() — including the Overlap and Jitter arguments, which nothing
      generated before this sample existed.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/FlowX.Compiler/FlowX.Compiler.csproj"
                      OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      Condition="'$(FlowXGeneratorDisabled)' != 'true'" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
